ns this turn.  \n\nTherefore, the best move is one that creates an *unblocked four* or *open three*.  \n\nE9 creates a four-in-a-row (closed at one end). That’s a strong threat because White must block the open left end (D9) next turn or Black wins at D9 afterward.  \n\nIs there an open three possible?  \nIf Black plays D9, then D9–E9(empty)–F9–G9–H9 is not straight because E9 empty. So not three.  \n\nWhat about playing H7? Makes H7–H8–H9 vertical three, but H10 is O below, so closed at bottom.  \n\nBest seems E9.  \n\n**Check legality:** E9 is not in occupied list, so valid.  \n\n---\n\n**Evaluation:**  \nE9 creates a horizontal four-in-a-row (closed on the right by I9), forcing White to block at D9 next move, giving Black initiative.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nIt’s the strongest direct threat available, and since White has no immediate winning threats, Black should build pressure toward a win.\n\n**Answer:**  \n{"col": "E", "row": 9}'}
